CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
The Protection of Women and Children from Acts of Violence, is implemented based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of Victims of Acts of Violence;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. the best interests of Victims of acts of violence; and
e. The principle of the right to life, survival and development,

Article 3
The objectives of the protection of women and children from acts of violence are to:
a. prevent violence against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children; d. provide services and Empowerment to women and children victims of violence, reporters and witnesses; e. facilitate and conduct mediation of domestic disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household; and
f. Facilitate the restoration of the rights, dignity and personal value of victims.

Article 5
The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect women from acts of violence that occur in public or private life in the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. economic violence;
d. social violence;
e. psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of freedom; and
h. threats of certain actions.

Article 16
(1) The Regional Government in conducting cooperation and coordination regarding the Protection of Women and Children from acts of violence, shall establish a Forum for the Care of Women and Children from Acts of Violence (FP2ATK);
(2) The membership of FP2ATK consists of elements:
a. regional government;
b. vertical agencies;
c. educational institutions;
d. health institutions;
e. social institutions;
f. labor institutions; and
g. community organizations that care about the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(3) The Organizational Structure of FP2ATK as referred to in paragraph (2) consists of:
a. policy formulation working group;
b. socialization working group;
b. service working group; and
c. data, information, monitoring and evaluation working group.
(4) The establishment and term of office of FP2ATK members as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be stipulated by Regent's Decree.

Part Three
Integrated Services for Victims of Acts of Violence Article 10 (1) The Regional Government implements integrated services for victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 5 letter b through activities: a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. counseling;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. social rehabilitation services;
f. legal aid and assistance services; and/or g. repatriation and reintegration. (2) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of: a. special service rooms;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and safe houses; and/or d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for victim recovery.
(3) The implementation of services for victims of acts of violence is carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government together with P2TP2A with other social institutions;
(4) P2TP2A and other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (3) may receive and send case referrals from other service units in a networked manner;
(5) Integrated services for victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) through paragraph (4) are implemented in accordance with standard operating procedures; (6) Provisions regarding Standard Operating Procedures as referred to in paragraph (5) are regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 21
(1) In certain circumstances, the Regional Government may conduct cooperation regarding women and children from acts of violence with:
a. other regional governments;
b. the police;
b. advocates;
C. health workers;
d. social workers;
e. volunteer companions;
f. spiritual counselors;
g. other law enforcers, to assist victims in the court hearing process;
h. the national commission on anti-violence against women; i. the Indonesian child protection commission or Child Protection Institution; and/or j certain parties desired for the benefit of the victim. (2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of: i
a. socialization;
b. provision of legal aid;
c. health services;
d. victim assistance;
e. counseling;
, £ social rehabilitation;
g. spiritual guidance; and/or
h. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 19
Further provisions regarding the mechanism and working procedures of FP2ATK shall be regulated by Regent Regulation. Article 20 (1) The Regional Government in the context of services for victims of acts of violence, shall establish P2TP2A. (2) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) shall have the duties of;
a. providing services, including:
1. receiving complaints/reports of violence against women and/or children, consultation, and counseling; 2. receiving and sending case referrals from Regency/City Regional Government or institutions established by the Regency/City Regional Government; 3. providing legal assistance;
4. health;
5. social rehabilitation;
6. legal services; and
7. repatriation and social reintegration.
b. providing protection to victims of acts of violence, reporters and witnesses. (3) The appointment of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Decree. (4) Regional Apparatus shall conduct guidance for P2TP2A in carrying out duties as referred to in paragraph (2).

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
The Protection of Women and Children from Acts of Violence, is implemented based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of Victims of Acts of Violence;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. the best interests of Victims of acts of violence; and
e. The principle of the right to life, survival and development,

Article 12
(1) The protection of children who are victims of acts of violence as referred to in article 5 letter a shall be the responsibility of the Regional Government. (2) The protection of child victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by means of: a. formulating policies for prevention, reduction of vulnerable risks, handling of victims and child data and information systems; b. providing maintenance, care, and social rehabilitation for neglected children, both within institutions and outside institutions;
c. providing shelters, maintenance and care for neglected children; and d. providing special protection to children.

